{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966806,"nonce":"ggxVeS7+LvOtTV+wHX+tBTaIgy8=","title":"Tooltip CSS fade-in keyframe makes text glyphs disappear because the glyph pipeline doesn't","message":"`.cn-tooltip` had a CSS `@keyframes cn-tooltip-enter { from { opacity: 0 } to { opacity: 1 } }` declaration intended to fade the tooltip in. The rule never actually fired before — class-only `@keyframes` animations didn't start until the `start_css_animation_for_class` fix in `start_all_css_animations` (commit 692243a1).\n\nRe-enabling the fade exposed a text-glyph opacity gap: the glyph rendering pipeline (separate from SDF) doesn't pick up the container's animated opacity mid-animation. Visible symptom: tooltip text disappears on desktop, looks laggy on web while the container fades in.\n\nWorkaround in place: `.cn-tooltip` reverted to snap-in (no `animation:`). Documented in the rule's comment in cn_styles.rs.\n\n**Why:** The glyph emit path reads `render_node.props.opacity` once at collection time but the CSS animation system writes opacity to the parent's props mid-frame, after the glyph batch has already been built.\n\n**How to apply:** Any CSS `animation:` that interpolates opacity on a container holding `text()` widgets will produce invisible text on desktop. Avoid opacity-only enter keyframes for surfaces that contain text glyphs (or animate transform/scale instead, which renders through SDF and propagates correctly). The proper fix is to make the glyph emit honour animated parent opacity.","files":null}]}